 "blurb": "Treats the shot as a photo print that has got wet. Water soaks in from a few places, mostly from the edges, with a ragged front. Inside the soaked part the dyes loosen and run outwards, so the picture fades, warms and smears, and the colour piles up in a brown tide line where the water stopped. Older tide lines stay where it paused before, and the swollen emulsion dries into a cockled, wrinkled skin that flakes off to bare paper in places near the edge. Key Spread, or set Soaks in over to watch the water creep in.",
